## Verdict
- Answer: “Screen time has been associated with ADHD in children, but the available evidence here does not establish that screen time causes ADHD.” [c1]
- Decision: Use wording that describes an association, not causation. The available material does not establish a causal effect.
- Confidence: medium - the claim is judged overstated, but the supporting evidence card returned no directly relevant document.
- What would change it: Direct evidence that measures screen exposure and ADHD in children and supports a causal relationship.

## What the data shows

The Prism assessment calls the causal claim “overstated” and says findings are mixed and show association, not direct causal evidence. [c1] It suggests describing screen time as “associated with” or “linked to” ADHD symptoms instead. [c1]

The evidence-card query found no document that speaks to the claim as written. [c2] The assessed corpus mix was 428 supporting, 820 qualifying, 62 contradicting, and 1,314 neutral documents. [c1] These are corpus classifications, not a count of studies demonstrating that screen time causes ADHD. The lane note says no papers covered both sides of the claim, and the material assessed concerns media consumption or screen-time exposure alone; it does not establish the connection asserted. [c1]

## Evidence

- Prism claim-improvement assessment. Prism MCP, year not provided, tool result [c1]. Supports the judgment that the causal wording is overstated and offers association-based rewrites. Quote: “The claim asserts causation, but evidence only shows association and mixed findings; no direct causal evidence is provided.”
- Prism evidence-card query. Prism MCP, year not provided, tool result [c2]. Supports the limitation that no directly relevant document was returned. Quote: “11 documents were read and none speaks to this claim as written.”

The source material provides no strongest grounded research document to cite for this claim. It therefore supports a cautious rewrite, not a conclusion that screen time causes ADHD—or that it cannot cause ADHD.

## Method and limits

The supplied Prism results have no date windows or end dates, so the gap to today (2026-10-23) cannot be determined. No study-level coverage, search dates, or document identifiers were supplied. The assessment used the “subject” lane; its note says no papers covered both sides of the claim. The evidence-card call returned no directly relevant document.

The corpus figures are Prism classifications from the claim-improvement result, not effect sizes, prevalence estimates, or counts of causal studies. The results do not specify the corpus’s full search and inclusion methods, so they cannot establish representativeness. The classification and wording judgment are Prism’s interpretation. They cannot answer whether screen time causes ADHD, how large any effect might be, or which kinds or amounts of screen use matter.

## References

No bibliographic research documents were returned. The two Prism tool results are cited as calls [c1] and [c2], not as entries in the reference list.

